    "lawCode": "Amd §§6802 & 6810, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4245\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the education law, in relation to electronic prescriptions and\ngeneric substitution\n\nPURPOSE OF GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nTo clarify the means by which\nphysicians and other authorized prescribers should transmit\nprescriptions electronically, particularly with respect to provisions\nrelating to the prescription of brand name drugs.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 of the bill adds a new\nsubdivision 23 to Section 6802 of the Education Law to define\n\"electronic prescription\" and \"electronic signature.\"\n\nSection 2 allows for the use of electronic prescriptions and clarifies\nhow a prescriber's electronic signature is used to validate an\nelectronic prescription and indicate whether a prescription is to be\n\"dispensed as written.\"\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nIt is important to ensure that current law governing\nprescriptions keeps pace with the increased use of electronic\nprescribing and remove any confusion or barriers to its expanded use.\nAs currently written, New York law cannot be entirely reconciled with\nelectronic prescriptions.\nThis bill would not alter existing policies related to prescriptions,\nbut would adapt those policies to the electronic prescribing context\nand ensure that existing rules governing prescriptions would continue\nto apply.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2010: A.11228-A - Referred to Higher Education\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
